------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

The GC31 cellular gateway keeps a site online over LTE. Firmware has to know the
current signal quality (to drive the LED bars, to decide when to fail over to
the second SIM) and also the recent history (the installer app shows "worst
signal in the last minute" so a tech can tell a marginal mount from a good one).

Signal quality comes from the modem over an AT channel:

    struct RssiSample {
        int      status;     // MODEM_ERROR / MODEM_BUSY / MODEM_OK
        int      rssi_dbm;   // received power, dBm, typically -115 .. -55
        int      sinr_db;    // signal/(interference+noise), dB
        uint64_t timestamp;  // microseconds, modem_now_us() clock
    };

    struct RssiSample modem_poll_rssi(void);

Behaviour of that one call:

    status        blocks            payload fields         how often
    ------------  ----------------  ---------------------  ---------------------
    MODEM_OK      0 .. 100 ms       fresh, valid           ~half the polls
    MODEM_BUSY    0 .. 100 ms       GARBAGE                ~half the polls
                                    ("no new value; the previous one still
                                      holds")
    MODEM_ERROR   returns at once   GARBAGE                occasionally (~6%)

    The FIRST call returns MODEM_OK immediately, without blocking.
    modem_poll_rssi() is NOT thread-safe: static state + its own RNG.
    Exactly one thread may ever call it.

    Scaled for the exercise: the real AT+CSQ round trip on this module blocks up
    to ~1 s; here it is 0..100 ms. The "last 60 s" window is compiled as 1.5 s
    (RSSI_WINDOW_US in rssi_window.h) so that eviction actually happens inside a
    ~7 second test run. Your code must not assume either number.

A blocking, single-threaded, sometimes-lying vendor call is not something the LED
task, the failover task and the installer app can each call. Wrap it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.  Non-blocking, thread-safe access to the latest value AND to its age.

    int  rssi_init(void);
    void rssi_deinit(void);

    int  rssi_get_latest(int *dbm, uint64_t *age_us);
    bool rssi_is_healthy(uint64_t max_age_us);

  rssi_get_latest() returns 0 and fills both out-params, or -1 if nothing has
  ever been sampled. It must never touch the modem and never block.

  The new part compared with the ALS problem: STALENESS. A value with no age is
  useless here — "-113 dBm" measured 40 ms ago means "we are on the edge of
  coverage", the same number measured 9 minutes ago means "the modem stopped
  answering and nobody noticed". So:
      * *dbm and *age_us must describe the SAME sample. A reader must not be
        able to combine a fresh value with an old timestamp, or the other way
        round, no matter when it reads.
      * rssi_is_healthy(max_age_us) reports whether the sampler is still alive:
        a valid sample exists and it is at most max_age_us old.

Part 2.  Sliding window statistics over the last RSSI_WINDOW_US.

    int    rssi_min_in_window(void);
    int    rssi_max_in_window(void);
    double rssi_avg_in_window(void);

  min and max must be AMORTISED O(1) per query, not O(number of samples in the
  window). A plain scan is the obvious first answer and it is the wrong one:
  the modem can hand you a value every 10 ms, so a 60 s window is 6000 samples,
  and the installer app polls both metrics several times a second while the
  radio task is appending. Keep the answer ready instead of recomputing it.

  The structure to reach for: a ring buffer of (timestamp, rssi) plus two
  MONOTONIC DEQUES of indices into it — one non-decreasing in rssi (its front
  is the window minimum) and one non-increasing (its front is the maximum).
  Eviction is by timestamp, at both append time and query time, because samples
  age out even when nothing new arrives.

  rssi_avg_in_window() is a running sum divided by the count — O(1) as well.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour (what the harness marks PASS/FAIL)

  * Getters called BEFORE rssi_init(): report "no data" (-1 / RSSI_NONE / NAN),
    do not crash, do not return garbage.
  * Right after rssi_init() returns, a value already exists and its age is a
    few ms at most — the first (non-blocking) poll was taken synchronously.
  * latest value and age match the ground truth the fake modem recorded.
  * min / max / avg match a brute-force O(n) reference computed from that ground
    truth, at several instants, including after older samples have been evicted.
  * The modem dies (the harness makes every poll return MODEM_BUSY): the last
    good value is still readable with a growing age, rssi_is_healthy(short)
    flips to false, and once the whole window has aged out min/max return
    RSSI_NONE and avg returns NAN. Health comes back when the modem recovers.
  * modem_poll_rssi() is called from exactly one thread, never concurrently.
  * 4 reader threads for 2 s see no value that the modem never produced, and no
    getter call takes anywhere near the 100 ms the modem can block for.
  * After rssi_deinit() the sampler thread is gone: the modem stops being polled,
    and rssi_deinit() itself returns in well under 500 ms.
  Last line of the run is either "ALL CHECKS PASSED (0 failed)" or "N CHECK(S)
  FAILED" (exit status 1). The stub as shipped fails ~12 checks and hangs nowhere.

