Things the interviewer is listening for  (answer these on paper before coding)

  1. Why can't rssi_get_latest() just call modem_poll_rssi()? How many threads
     may call the vendor API, and what breaks if two do?
  2. What does "non-blocking" mean for a reader here — never waiting on the
     modem, or never taking a lock at all? Which of your getters still takes a
     lock, and what is the longest it can be held?
  3. The reader needs a value and its timestamp TOGETHER. What exactly goes
     wrong if you keep them in two variables and read them one after the other?
     Name the failure, and say which direction of skew each interleaving gives.
  4. You have one 64-bit atomic. What do you pack into it so that a single load
     yields value + age? What are the limits of that packing (range, resolution,
     lifetime), and at what point must you move to a seqlock or a double buffer?
  5. Is a plain `int` load/store atomic on ARM64 in practice? Why is "in
     practice" not an acceptable answer in C? (tearing, reordering, what the
     compiler is allowed to do to a non-atomic shared variable)
  6. Part 2: how much memory does a 60 s window cost if a new value can arrive
     every 10 ms? What is your capacity, and what do you do when it is full?
  7. Part 2: why is a scan for min/max wrong at scale, and what is the invariant
     of the monotonic deque that replaces it? Why is a sample that is smaller
     than the one before it allowed to delete its predecessor forever?
  8. Part 2: why TWO deques? What do they store — values or indices — and why?
     Show that each sample is pushed once and popped at most once (so the
     per-query cost really is amortised O(1)).
  9. MODEM_BUSY says "nothing new, the old value still holds"; MODEM_ERROR says
     "the payload is garbage". What does each one do to the latest value, to the
     window, and to health? Why must ERROR make you back off?
 10. The modem firmware wedges and stops producing values. What should
     rssi_get_latest(), rssi_is_healthy() and min/max/avg each return, before and
     after the window has fully aged out? And how do you stop the sampler at
     shutdown when it may be parked inside a 100 ms vendor call?
